=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nft/iptables `FORWARD` chain on router6, specifically a set of DROP rules gated on `iifname "eth2"` — the eth2 interface carries the r6_r3 inter-site backbone link (10.50.1.0/24) connecting router6 to router3 and the rest of the upstream network.

**Mechanism:** Router6 is the sole gateway to Location C (172.18.51.0/24 on eth1, 172.18.50.0/24 on eth0) via mswitch2. All traffic from external sites must enter router6 on eth2 and be forwarded to eth1/eth0 toward Location C. The FORWARD chain has three DROP rules that collectively block every protocol entering from eth2 — TCP (with a single narrow ACCEPT exception for TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1), UDP, and a final catch-all that has already accumulated 44 dropped packets. Because the rules apply to the FORWARD chain (not INPUT), router6's own locally-generated pings to pc15 succeed — they bypass FORWARD entirely — creating the illusion that the last hop works while all transit traffic is silently discarded.

**Localization:** router6, nft/iptables FORWARD chain, ingress interface eth2 (r6_r3 link, 10.50.1.1↔10.50.1.2). All routers upstream of router6 (router0 through router3) carry valid static kernel routes to 172.18.51.0/24 pointing through router6, and the r6_r3 link itself is healthy (0% loss on direct pings between 10.50.1.1 and 10.50.1.2). The block is entirely at router6's forwarding filter.

**Root cause:** An overly restrictive nft FORWARD ACL on router6 drops all transit ICMP, TCP, and UDP traffic arriving from the inter-site backbone interface (eth2), making Location C unreachable from every upstream site including Location A.

**Fix:** Remove (or correctly scope) the three DROP rules under `iifname "eth2"` in router6's nft `filter FORWARD` chain, allowing legitimate ICMP and routed IP traffic to be forwarded from the backbone link to the Location C subnets.
=== END ===